window.CNOTES_INDEX = [{"page":"index.html","title":"C Notes — Learn C Programming Offline","group":"Tutorial","url":"index.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Notes — Learn C Programming Offline","text":"Free · Offline · No account Learn C Programming C is the language behind operating systems, embedded devices, databases and much of the software you use every day. This tutorial takes you from your first program to pointers, structures, files and dynamic memory — with examples you can run, edit and study right here. Start with C Introduction → Run C in your browser Cheat sheet 0 of 22 chapters complete 0% complete Reset progress Continue learning"},{"id":"start-here","h":"Start here","text":"New to C? Go in order. Each chapter is short, has examples you can run in the built-in editor, and ends with a link to the next topic. Your progress is saved in this browser. 01 C Introduction What C is, where it came from and why it is still worth learning. 02 C Get Started Install a compiler and write your first program. 03 C Syntax Statements, semicolons, braces and the shape of a C program. 04 C Output Print text, numbers and formatted values with printf. 05 C Variables Store and change values with named variables. 06 C Data Types int, float, double, char and their size and limits."},{"id":"the-full-tutorial","h":"The full tutorial","text":"C Comments Document code and temporarily disable it. C Constants const and #define — values that must not change. C Operators Arithmetic, comparison, logic, assignment and bitwise. C If...Else Make decisions with conditions. C Switch Choose between many fixed cases. C Loops while, for, do-while, break and continue. C Arrays Store many values of the same type. C Strings Text as arrays of characters, plus string.h. C User Input Read values from the keyboard with scanf and fgets. C Functions Break programs into reusable blocks. C Pointers Variables that hold memory addresses. C Structures Group related values into your own types. C Unions & Enums Shared storage and named integer constants. C File Handling Read and write files with FILE and fopen. C Memory malloc, calloc, realloc and free. C Preprocessor #include, #define and conditional compilation. C Reference Keywords, format specifiers and library functions. C Examples Complete programs you can study and copy. C Quiz Fifteen questions with explanations."},{"id":"practice-reference-and-tools","h":"Practice, reference and tools","text":"Reading is not enough. Run the code, solve the exercises, test yourself, and keep the reference open while you write. C Exercises 18 graded exercises with full solutions and progress tracking. C Quiz Fifteen questions with explanations and a saved best score. C Cheat Sheet Types, operators, pointers, memory, files and the standard library on one printable page. C Glossary Plain-English definitions of the terms you meet in C. C Certificate Complete the course and print a personal certificate. Search Full-text search across every chapter, example and reference table. Bookmarks Save pages to revisit; stored locally in your browser. Try It Editor Write C, run it for real, and see the output — fully offline."},{"id":"how-this-site-works","h":"How this site works","text":"Every page follows the same pattern: a short explanation, one or more code examples, and notes about the mistakes beginners make. The Try it button opens the built-in editor with that example loaded. Press Run to compile and execute it with PicoC , a small C interpreter compiled to WebAssembly — real output, real input through scanf , and no server involved. Offline first. This site is a set of plain HTML, CSS, JavaScript and WebAssembly files. There are no third-party scripts, no fonts loaded from the internet and no tracking. Save the folder to a USB drive or open index.html directly in any modern browser. When served over HTTP(S), it can also be installed as a standalone app and cached for offline use."},{"id":"built-in-study-tools","h":"Built-in study tools","text":"Real C execution. The editor runs a C interpreter locally; programs are stopped after 10 seconds if they loop forever. Progress tracking. Mark chapters complete, see the percentage in the sidebar, and solve exercises with saved ticks. Quiz and certificate. Your best quiz score is saved, and the certificate unlocks when all chapters and the quiz are complete. Search and bookmarks. Full-text search over the whole site, plus a personal bookmark list. Dark mode, keyboard shortcuts, print styles. Press ? anywhere to see the shortcuts. One honest limitation. PicoC supports a practical subset of C89/C99. A few valid standard-C constructs — such as aggregate initialisers for structs, very complex macros and variable-length arrays — may be rejected. For production code and full standard C, use GCC or Clang as described in C Get Started ."},{"id":"why-learn-c","h":"Why learn C?","text":"It is small and honest. C has around 40 keywords. Once you understand C, very little about it is hidden from you. It teaches how computers work. Pointers, memory layout and manual allocation build a mental model that higher-level languages often hide. It is everywhere. Linux, Windows, Git, SQLite, Python's interpreter, and most embedded firmware are written in C. It is the base for C++, C#, Java and Go. Their syntax and many concepts descend from C."},{"id":"what-you-need","h":"What you need","text":"A C compiler and a text editor. On Windows, the easiest route is GCC via MSYS2 or Visual Studio Community ; on macOS, Xcode Command Line Tools; on Linux, the build-essential / gcc package. The next chapter walks through it. Tip: press Ctrl + K (or ⌘ + K ) to search all content, B to bookmark a page, T to switch themes, and ? for the full shortcut list."}]},{"page":"c-intro.html","title":"C Introduction","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-intro.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Introduction","text":"C Tutorial › Introduction C Introduction C is a general-purpose, compiled programming language created by Dennis Ritchie at Bell Labs between 1969 and 1973. It was designed for writing the Unix operating system — and it is still one of the most influential languages ever made."},{"id":"what-is-c","h":"What is C?","text":"C is a procedural language: a program is a collection of functions, and execution starts at main() . It is statically typed (every variable has a type known at compile time), compiled (source code is translated to machine code before it runs) and low-level enough to work directly with memory through pointers. Despite being small, C is powerful. The same language can write a tiny microcontroller program and a large operating system kernel. Output"},{"id":"why-is-c-called-a-middle-level-language","h":"Why is C called a \"middle-level\" language?","text":"It combines features of high-level languages (functions, loops, structured code) with low-level access (raw memory, bit operations, pointers). That combination is why C is used for both application code and system code."},{"id":"a-very-short-history","h":"A very short history","text":"Year Event 1972 Dennis Ritchie develops C at Bell Labs to rewrite Unix. 1978 The C Programming Language by Kernighan and Ritchie (\"K&R C\") is published. 1989/1990 ANSI/ISO standardises C — often called C89 or C90 . 1999 C99 adds // comments, long long , variable-length arrays and more. 2011 C11 adds threads, atomics and anonymous structs/unions. 2018 C17 — mostly bug fixes and clarifications. 2024 C23 is published with new attributes, typeof , constexpr and more."},{"id":"where-c-is-used","h":"Where C is used","text":"Operating systems — Linux, Windows, macOS and Android kernels. Embedded systems — cars, routers, washing machines, medical devices. Databases — SQLite, PostgreSQL and MySQL are largely C/C++. Language runtimes — CPython, Ruby MRI and the JVM are written in C/C++. Tools — Git, curl, the core of most command-line utilities. Libraries — OpenSSL, zlib, libpng, FFmpeg and thousands more."},{"id":"what-makes-c-different","h":"What makes C different?","text":"Manual memory management. You allocate memory with malloc and release it with free . Nothing happens automatically, which is both freedom and responsibility. Pointers. Variables that store addresses. They enable efficient data structures and are the source of many bugs when used carelessly. No classes, exceptions or garbage collector. C keeps the language small. Libraries provide the rest. Undefined behaviour. Some mistakes (reading past an array, using freed memory) are not required to produce an error — the behaviour is simply undefined. Learning what not to do matters."},{"id":"your-first-program-explained","h":"Your first program explained","text":"#include <stdio.h> — a preprocessor directive that includes the standard input/output header. int main(void) — the entry point. int is the value returned to the operating system. printf(...) — prints formatted text to standard output. \\n — an escape sequence meaning \"new line\". return 0; — tells the shell the program succeeded. ; — ends a statement. Almost every C statement needs one."},{"id":"c-vs-other-languages","h":"C vs. other languages","text":"Feature C Python Java Execution Compiled to machine code Interpreted Compiled to bytecode (JVM) Typing Static, explicit Dynamic Static, explicit Memory Manual Garbage collected Garbage collected Speed Very fast Slower Fast after warm-up Typical use Systems, embedded Scripts, data science Business applications Note: this tutorial targets standard C as described by C99 and later. Code will compile with GCC, Clang, MSVC (with minor warnings) and TCC. Features added in C11/C23 are marked when discussed."},{"id":"ready-to-start","h":"Ready to start?","text":"Next, install a compiler and build your first program."}]},{"page":"c-setup.html","title":"C Get Started","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-setup.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Get Started","text":"C Tutorial › Get Started C Get Started To write and run C you need two things: a compiler that turns source code into a program, and a text editor to write it in. This chapter shows the fastest setup on Windows, macOS and Linux."},{"id":"step-1-install-a-compiler","h":"Step 1 — Install a compiler","text":"Windows: GCC with MSYS2 (recommended) Download and install MSYS2 from msys2.org . Open the MSYS2 UCRT64 terminal and run: pacman -S mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-gcc Add C:\\msys64\\ucrt64\\bin to your PATH. Open a new Command Prompt and check: gcc --version Windows: Visual Studio Install Visual Studio Community with the Desktop development with C++ workload. Use the Developer Command Prompt and compile with cl hello.c . macOS Open Terminal and run xcode-select --install . This installs Clang, the compiler Apple ships. Verify with clang --version . Linux (Debian / Ubuntu) On Fedora use sudo dnf install gcc ; on Arch use sudo pacman -S gcc . No installation? Use an online compiler If you cannot install anything, sites such as onlinegdb.com or godbolt.org can compile C in the browser. They require internet access, so they are only a fallback for this offline course."},{"id":"step-2-choose-an-editor","h":"Step 2 — Choose an editor","text":"VS Code + the C/C++ extension — cross-platform and popular. Code::Blocks or Dev-C++ — simple IDE bundles for beginners. CLion — a full commercial IDE. Vim, Neovim, Emacs — fast terminal editors if you prefer the keyboard. Any plain text editor works. Do not use a word processor such as Word."},{"id":"step-3-write-your-first-program","h":"Step 3 — Write your first program","text":"Create a folder called c-projects and inside it a file named hello.c : Output"},{"id":"step-4-compile-and-run","h":"Step 4 — Compile and run","text":"Open a terminal in the folder that contains hello.c . GCC / Clang The -o hello option names the output file. Without it, GCC writes a.out (or a.exe on Windows). MSVC Useful compiler flags Flag Meaning -Wall Enable common warnings. -Wextra Enable extra warnings. -Werror Treat warnings as errors. -std=c11 / -std=c17 Choose the language standard. -g Include debug information (for GDB). -O2 Optimise for speed. -lm Link the maths library (needed for sqrt etc. on Linux)."},{"id":"the-compile-link-run-pipeline","h":"The compile → link → run pipeline","text":"Preprocessing — #include and #define are expanded. Compilation — C source becomes assembly, then object code ( .o / .obj ). Linking — your object files are joined with libraries to make an executable. Execution — the operating system loads the program and calls main ."},{"id":"common-first-run-problems","h":"Common first-run problems","text":"Message Fix 'gcc' is not recognized GCC is not installed or its bin folder is not on PATH. Reopen the terminal after editing PATH. stdio.h: No such file or directory The compiler's standard library is missing — reinstall the toolchain. undefined reference to 'main' You compiled the wrong file, or main is misspelled. expected ';' before ... A semicolon is missing on the previous line. Permission denied when running Run ./hello , not hello , on Linux/macOS, and mark it executable with chmod +x hello . Tip: compile with gcc -Wall -Wextra -std=c17 hello.c -o hello from day one. The warnings catch real bugs."},{"id":"try-it-in-this-site","h":"Try it in this site","text":"Open the built-in Try It editor to write, run, and download C files. It uses a small C interpreter compiled to WebAssembly, so most tutorial programs run directly in the browser — no compiler installation required."}]},{"page":"c-syntax.html","title":"C Syntax","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-syntax.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Syntax","text":"C Tutorial › Syntax C Syntax C syntax is the set of rules that defines how a C program is written. It is small and consistent: statements end with semicolons, blocks are wrapped in braces, and case matters."},{"id":"the-smallest-c-program","h":"The smallest C program","text":"Output"},{"id":"statements-and-semicolons","h":"Statements and semicolons","text":"A statement is one instruction. In C, most statements end with a semicolon: Forgetting the semicolon is the most common compile error. The compiler often reports it on the next line, because that is where it notices the statement never finished."},{"id":"blocks-and-braces","h":"Blocks and braces","text":"Braces { } group statements into a block . Function bodies, loops, conditions and structures all use blocks."},{"id":"case-sensitivity","h":"Case sensitivity","text":"C is case-sensitive. age , Age and AGE are three different names. Keywords are always lowercase: int , not Int ."},{"id":"identifiers","h":"Identifiers","text":"Identifiers are names you give to variables, functions, structures and so on. The rules: May contain letters, digits and underscores. Must begin with a letter or an underscore (not a digit). Cannot be a keyword such as int or return . Are case-sensitive. Naming conventions snake_case for variables and functions: total_score , read_file . UPPER_CASE for macros and constants: MAX_SIZE , PI . Descriptive names beat short names: student_count is clearer than n ."},{"id":"whitespace-and-indentation","h":"Whitespace and indentation","text":"Spaces, tabs and newlines are ignored by the compiler (except inside strings and to separate tokens). Indentation is for humans, but consistent indentation makes bugs visible. Four spaces per level is a common C style."},{"id":"comments-in-brief","h":"Comments in brief","text":"Comments start with // (to end of line) or wrap with /* ... */ . They are ignored by the compiler. Read the full Comments chapter for details."},{"id":"the-main-function","h":"The main function","text":"Every C program must have exactly one main . Execution starts there. The standard forms are: The return 0; value is passed to the operating system. Zero means success."},{"id":"preprocessor-directives","h":"Preprocessor directives","text":"Lines starting with # are handled before compilation. They do not end with a semicolon. Output"},{"id":"common-syntax-mistakes","h":"Common syntax mistakes","text":"Mistake Example Correction Missing semicolon int x = 5 int x = 5; Assignment in a condition if (x = 5) if (x == 5) Using a name before declaring it printf(\"%d\", y); Declare y first. Mismatched braces if (x) { ... Close every { with } . Wrong quotes printf(“Hi”); Use straight quotes: \"Hi\" . Tip: let your editor auto-indent and auto-close braces. Compile often — small programs are much easier to debug."}]},{"page":"c-output.html","title":"C Output","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-output.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Output","text":"C Tutorial › Output C Output C prints output with functions from <stdio.h> . printf is the workhorse: it formats values and writes them to the screen."},{"id":"printf","h":"printf","text":"The name means print formatted . The first argument is a format string; the rest are the values to insert. Output"},{"id":"format-specifiers","h":"Format specifiers","text":"A % followed by a letter is a placeholder. The matching argument replaces it. Output Common specifiers Specifier Type Example output %d / %i int 42 %u unsigned int 42 %ld / %lu long / unsigned long 1000000 %lld long long 9000000000 %f float / double 3.140000 %.2f float with 2 decimals 3.14 %e scientific notation 3.140000e+00 %c char A %s string Hello %x / %X hexadecimal ff / FF %o octal 377 %p pointer address 0x7ffd... %% a literal percent sign %"},{"id":"printing-several-values","h":"Printing several values","text":"Output The order of the arguments must match the order of the specifiers. A mismatch such as passing a double to %d is undefined behaviour — it may print garbage or crash."},{"id":"escape-sequences","h":"Escape sequences","text":"These special character pairs start with a backslash and insert characters that are hard to type. Sequence Meaning \\n New line \\t Horizontal tab \\\\ Backslash \\\" Double quote \\' Single quote \\0 Null character (ends a string) \\r Carriage return \\a Alert / bell \\b Backspace \\f Form feed \\v Vertical tab Output"},{"id":"width-precision-and-padding","h":"Width, precision and padding","text":"You can control how wide a field is and how many decimal places are shown. Output"},{"id":"puts-and-putchar","h":"puts and putchar","text":"puts prints a string followed by a newline. putchar prints one character. They are simpler and slightly faster than printf when you do not need formatting. Output"},{"id":"output-buffering","h":"Output buffering","text":"printf writes into a buffer that is flushed when it fills, when the program ends, or when you print a newline on a terminal. If your program crashes before flushing, some output may be lost. fflush(stdout); forces a flush. Note: standard output is normally line-buffered on a terminal and fully buffered when redirected to a file. Errors should go to stderr with fprintf(stderr, ...) ."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Print your name, age and city on three separate lines. Print a table of five numbers, each right-aligned in a width of 8. Print the value of 22.0 / 7.0 with exactly five decimal places."}]},{"page":"c-comments.html","title":"C Comments","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-comments.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Comments","text":"C Tutorial › Comments C Comments Comments are notes for humans. The compiler ignores them completely, so they cost nothing at run time and make code easier to understand."},{"id":"single-line-comments","h":"Single-line comments","text":"Start with // . Everything until the end of the line is a comment. This style was added to C in C99, but compilers supported it long before. Output"},{"id":"multi-line-comments","h":"Multi-line comments","text":"Wrap text in /* and */ . This works everywhere, including inside a line of code and in C89."},{"id":"what-comments-are-for","h":"What comments are for","text":"Explaining why code does something, not what it does. The code already says what. Documenting functions — purpose, parameters, return value. Temporarily disabling code while debugging. Marking work with TODO or FIXME . File headers — author, date, licence."},{"id":"can-comments-be-nested","h":"Can comments be nested?","text":"No. Multi-line comments do not nest. The first */ ends the comment, even if an inner /* appeared earlier. The text after the closing */ is parsed as C and will cause an error. Use // on each line for commenting out blocks containing multi-line comments."},{"id":"commented-out-code","h":"Commented-out code","text":"You can comment out code to disable it: Do not leave large blocks of dead code in finished programs — use version control (Git) to keep old versions instead."},{"id":"preprocessor-comments","h":"Preprocessor comments","text":"Comments are removed before the preprocessor runs, so you cannot use them inside a #define in tricky ways. Keep macro definitions simple and comment above the line."},{"id":"quick-summary","h":"Quick summary","text":"Style Syntax Ends at Typical use Single line // text End of line Short notes, inline explanations Multi-line /* text */ First */ Headers, long explanations, disabling blocks Tip: write the comment before the code when the reason is not obvious. If the code needs a comment to explain what it does, consider renaming variables or splitting the function."}]},{"page":"c-variables.html","title":"C Variables","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-variables.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Variables","text":"C Tutorial › Variables C Variables A variable is a named container for a value. Every variable in C has a type that decides what kind of value it can hold and how much memory it uses."},{"id":"declaring-a-variable","h":"Declaring a variable","text":"Declaration tells the compiler the variable's name and type: At this point the value is indeterminate — it contains whatever happened to be in that memory. Never read a variable before assigning it."},{"id":"assigning-a-value","h":"Assigning a value","text":"Output"},{"id":"initialising-at-declaration","h":"Initialising at declaration","text":"You can combine declaration and assignment. This is the safest habit. Output"},{"id":"changing-a-variable","h":"Changing a variable","text":"Assignment replaces the old value. The variable keeps its type. Output"},{"id":"naming-rules","h":"Naming rules","text":"Letters, digits and underscores only. Must start with a letter or underscore. Case-sensitive: total and Total differ. Cannot be a C keyword. Should describe the value: item_count , not ic ."},{"id":"variables-and-format-specifiers","h":"Variables and format specifiers","text":"Use the specifier that matches the type, or printf will read the wrong bytes. Type Specifier Example int %d int n = 7; unsigned int %u unsigned int n = 7; long %ld long n = 100000L; float %f float f = 3.5f; double %f / %lf (scanf) double d = 3.5; char %c char c = 'A'; string %s char s[] = \"Hi\";"},{"id":"local-and-global-variables","h":"Local and global variables","text":"A variable declared inside a function is local : it exists only while that function runs. A variable declared outside every function is global : it exists for the whole program and can be used by any function. Output Prefer local variables. Globals make it hard to see which code changes a value."},{"id":"constants","h":"Constants","text":"Use const when a value must not change: See the Constants chapter for #define and literal rules. Warning: reading an uninitialised local variable is undefined behaviour. Some compilers with -Wall will warn you; always initialise."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Declare variables for your name, age, height and favourite letter, then print them. Create an int called lives set to 3, subtract 1, and print the result. Predict what happens if you assign 3.99 to an int . Test it with a compiler."}]},{"page":"c-datatypes.html","title":"C Data Types","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-datatypes.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Data Types","text":"C Tutorial › Data Types C Data Types A data type tells the compiler what kind of value a variable stores and how many bytes to reserve. C has a small set of built-in types plus the ability to build your own from structures, unions and enums."},{"id":"the-basic-types","h":"The basic types","text":"Type Typical size Holds Format char 1 byte A single character or small integer %c int 2 or 4 bytes Whole numbers %d short usually 2 bytes Smaller whole numbers %hd long 4 or 8 bytes Larger whole numbers %ld long long at least 8 bytes Very large whole numbers %lld float 4 bytes Decimal numbers, ~7 digits precision %f double 8 bytes Decimal numbers, ~15 digits precision %lf void none No value / generic pointer — Important: the C standard does not fix most sizes. It guarantees only minimum ranges. Use sizeof and <stdint.h> when exact sizes matter."},{"id":"example-the-basic-types","h":"Example: the basic types","text":"Output"},{"id":"how-big-is-a-type-sizeof","h":"How big is a type? sizeof","text":"sizeof is an operator that returns the size in bytes. Use %zu to print it. Output On a typical 64-bit system. On 32-bit systems long and pointers are usually 4 bytes."},{"id":"ranges-and-limits-h","h":"Ranges and limits.h","text":"Output"},{"id":"signed-and-unsigned","h":"Signed and unsigned","text":"Integer types can be signed (default, includes negatives) or unsigned (only zero and positives, with twice the positive range). Unsigned overflow wraps modulo 2 n ; signed overflow is undefined behaviour. Be careful when subtracting unsigned values."},{"id":"float-vs-double","h":"float vs double","text":"float uses 4 bytes and about 7 significant decimal digits. double uses 8 bytes and about 15. Use double for most calculations and float when memory matters (large arrays, graphics, embedded). Output Floating-point numbers are approximations. Do not compare them with == ; compare the difference to a small epsilon instead."},{"id":"type-conversion-and-casting","h":"Type conversion and casting","text":"C converts between types automatically when needed (implicit conversion), and you can force a conversion with a cast. Output"},{"id":"boolean-values","h":"Boolean values","text":"C traditionally uses int for truth: 0 is false, any non-zero value is true . C99 added <stdbool.h> with bool , true and false . Output"},{"id":"fixed-width-integers-stdint-h","h":"Fixed-width integers (stdint.h)","text":"When the exact number of bits matters — file formats, networking, hardware — use the typedefs from <stdint.h> :"},{"id":"derived-and-user-defined-types","h":"Derived and user-defined types","text":"Arrays — many values of one type: int scores[10]; Pointers — hold addresses: int *p; Functions — have a return type and parameter types. Structures — group different types: struct Point { int x, y; }; Unions — members share storage. Enums — named integer constants. typedef — an alias for an existing type. Tip: choose the simplest type that fits. Use int for ordinary counters, double for maths, size_t for sizes and indexes, and fixed-width types only when the exact width matters."}]},{"page":"c-constants.html","title":"C Constants","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-constants.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Constants","text":"C Tutorial › Constants C Constants A constant is a value that must not change while the program runs. C gives you three ways to express one: literal values, const variables, and #define macros."},{"id":"literal-constants","h":"Literal constants","text":"A literal is a value written directly in the source code. Integer suffixes Suffix Type Example u / U unsigned 4000000000u l / L long 1000000L ll / LL long long 9000000000LL ul / ULL unsigned long / long long 18446744073709551615ULL Character escapes"},{"id":"const-variables","h":"const variables","text":"Adding const makes a variable read-only. The compiler rejects any attempt to assign to it. Output const variables have a type, so the compiler can check them and a debugger can see them. They also follow normal scope rules. const pointers The position of const changes its meaning: Read declarations right-to-left: \"p1 is a pointer to a const int\", \"p2 is a const pointer to int\"."},{"id":"define-macros","h":"#define macros","text":"The preprocessor replaces text before the compiler sees it. A macro is not a variable — it has no type, no scope and no memory. Output Macros with arguments The extra parentheses are essential. Without them, SQUARE(2 + 3) would expand to 2 + 3 * 2 + 3 and produce 11 instead of 25."},{"id":"const-vs-define","h":"const vs #define","text":"const #define Handled by Compiler Preprocessor Has a type Yes No Debugger visible Yes Usually no Scope Normal scope rules From definition to end of file Works for types/headers No Yes Can take arguments No Yes (text substitution) Preferred for Values, function parameters Include guards, conditional compilation, constants in array sizes Tip: prefer const (or enum ) over object-like macros. Use macros for include guards and conditional compilation, where the preprocessor is actually needed."},{"id":"enum-constants","h":"enum constants","text":"An enumeration creates a set of named integer constants. It is the clean way to express a fixed list of values. Output See Unions & Enums for more."},{"id":"common-mistakes","h":"Common mistakes","text":"Missing parentheses in macros — #define HALF(x) x/2 breaks for expressions. Semicolon after a macro — #define MAX 100; puts a semicolon into every expansion. Do not add one. Trying to modify a const — the compiler stops you; casting away const and writing is undefined behaviour. Assuming const means compile-time constant — in C it means read-only. For array sizes, use an enum constant, a macro, or (C99+) a variable-length array."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Define const double GRAVITY = 9.81; and print an object's weight for masses 1 to 5 kg. Write a SQUARE macro and test it with SQUARE(2 + 3) . Create an enum for the days of the week and print Wednesday's numeric value."}]},{"page":"c-operators.html","title":"C Operators","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-operators.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Operators","text":"C Tutorial › Operators C Operators Operators are symbols that perform operations on values. C groups them into arithmetic, assignment, comparison, logical, bitwise and a few special ones."},{"id":"arithmetic-operators","h":"Arithmetic operators","text":"Operator Name Example (a = 10, b = 3) Result + Addition a + b 13 - Subtraction a - b 7 * Multiplication a * b 30 / Division a / b 3 (integer division) % Modulus (remainder) a % b 1 Output Integer division truncates. 7 / 2 is 3 , not 3.5 . Use a floating-point type: 7.0 / 2 or (double)7 / 2 . The % operator works only with integers."},{"id":"assignment-operators","h":"Assignment operators","text":"Operator Example Equivalent to = x = 5 Store 5 in x += x += 5 x = x + 5 -= x -= 5 x = x - 5 *= x *= 5 x = x * 5 /= x /= 5 x = x / 5 %= x %= 5 x = x % 5 &= |= ^= x |= 2 x = x | 2 <<= >>= x <<= 1 x = x << 1 Output"},{"id":"increment-and-decrement","h":"Increment and decrement","text":"++ adds one; -- subtracts one. Their position matters: Output Note: avoid modifying the same variable more than once in a single expression, as in i = i++ + ++i . That is undefined behaviour."},{"id":"comparison-operators","h":"Comparison operators","text":"Operator Meaning Example (a = 5, b = 8) Result == Equal to a == b 0 (false) != Not equal to a != b 1 (true) > Greater than a > b 0 < Less than a < b 1 >= Greater or equal a >= b 0 <= Less or equal a <= b 1 Comparison operators return 1 for true and 0 for false. Do not confuse = with == . if (x = 5) assigns 5 and is always true. if (x == 5) compares. Compile with -Wall to catch this."},{"id":"logical-operators","h":"Logical operators","text":"Operator Name True when && AND Both sides are true || OR At least one side is true ! NOT The value is false (0) && and || short-circuit: the right side is not evaluated if the result is already known."},{"id":"bitwise-operators","h":"Bitwise operators","text":"These work on individual bits of integers. They are common in systems programming, flags and embedded code. Operator Name Example (a = 6, b = 3) Result & Bitwise AND a & b 2 | Bitwise OR a | b 7 ^ Bitwise XOR a ^ b 5 ~ Bitwise NOT ~a -7 << Shift left a << 1 12 >> Shift right a >> 1 3 Output Shifting left by n multiplies by 2 n ; shifting right divides by 2 n (for non-negative values)."},{"id":"other-operators","h":"Other operators","text":"Operator Name Example sizeof Size in bytes sizeof(int) & Address of &x * Dereference *p ? : Ternary conditional x > 0 ? 1 : -1 . Structure member p.x -> Member through pointer p->x , Comma for (i = 0, j = 10; ...)"},{"id":"precedence","h":"Precedence","text":"Operators with higher precedence bind first. When in doubt, add parentheses — they cost nothing and prevent bugs. Level Operators Associativity 1 (highest) () [] . -> ++ -- (postfix) Left to right 2 ! ~ ++ -- (prefix) + - (unary) * & sizeof (type) Right to left 3 * / % Left to right 4 + - Left to right 5 << >> Left to right 6 < <= > >= Left to right 7 == != Left to right 8 & Left to right 9 ^ Left to right 10 | Left to right 11 && Left to right 12 || Left to right 13 ?: Right to left 14 = += -= etc. Right to left 15 (lowest) , Left to right"},{"id":"common-mistakes","h":"Common mistakes","text":"= instead of == in conditions. Forgetting that / between integers truncates. Using % with floats (not allowed). Dividing by zero (undefined behaviour; the program may crash). Assuming & means logical AND — it is bitwise AND. Confusing a & b == c with (a & b) == c — equality binds tighter."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Ask for two integers and print their sum, difference, product, quotient and remainder. Use a ternary operator to print \"even\" or \"odd\" for a number. Set bits 0 and 3 of an integer using |= , then print the result in hex. Predict the result of 5 + 2 * 3 - 4 / 2 before running it."}]},{"page":"c-conditions.html","title":"C If...Else","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-conditions.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C If...Else","text":"C Tutorial › If...Else C If...Else Conditions let a program choose between different paths. In C, 0 is false and any non-zero value is true ."},{"id":"the-if-statement","h":"The if statement","text":"Run a block only when a condition is true. Output The condition must be in parentheses. The braces are optional for a single statement, but always using them prevents mistakes."},{"id":"if-else","h":"if...else","text":"Provide a second path when the condition is false. Output"},{"id":"if-else-if-else","h":"if...else if...else","text":"Test several conditions in order. The first true branch wins; the rest are skipped. Output Order matters. If you tested score >= 60 first, every score above 60 would get a D."},{"id":"comparison-and-logical-operators","h":"Comparison and logical operators","text":"Operator Meaning Example == equal x == 10 != not equal x != 10 > < greater / less x > 0 >= <= greater/less or equal x <= 100 && AND — both true x > 0 && x < 10 || OR — at least one true x == 0 || x == 1 ! NOT — reverses truth !found"},{"id":"nested-conditions","h":"Nested conditions","text":"An if can contain another if . Keep nesting shallow; deep nesting is hard to read. A flatter version with && is often clearer:"},{"id":"the-ternary-operator","h":"The ternary operator","text":"A compact one-line choice: condition ? value_if_true : value_if_false . Output Use the ternary form for short, simple choices. For anything longer, use if...else ."},{"id":"common-mistakes","h":"Common mistakes","text":"Mistake Why it is wrong Fix if (x = 5) Assigns 5; always true. if (x == 5) if (x > 1 && < 10) Each side needs its own variable. if (x > 1 && x < 10) if (x > 1 || x < 10) Always true — almost every number matches. Use && for a range. if (x == 1, 2) Comma operator; only the last value counts. if (x == 1 || x == 2) Missing braces with multiple statements Only the first statement is conditional. Always use { } . else if after else Syntax error. Test conditions before the final else ."},{"id":"complete-example-a-simple-calculator-check","h":"Complete example: a simple calculator check","text":"Output Tip: when you have many fixed options to compare against one value, a switch is usually cleaner than a long if...else if chain."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Read a number and print whether it is positive, negative or zero. Read a year and print whether it is a leap year. Read three numbers and print the largest. Assign a letter grade from a score using if...else if ."}]},{"page":"c-switch.html","title":"C Switch","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-switch.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Switch","text":"C Tutorial › Switch C Switch The switch statement selects one block from many options based on the value of a single integer or character expression."},{"id":"basic-example","h":"Basic example","text":"Output"},{"id":"why-break-matters","h":"Why break matters","text":"Without break , execution falls through into the next case. This is sometimes useful, but it is usually a bug. Output Intentional fall-through can group cases. Add a comment such as /* fall through */ so readers know it is deliberate."},{"id":"grouping-cases","h":"Grouping cases","text":"Output"},{"id":"the-default-case","h":"The default case","text":"default runs when no case matches. It can appear anywhere, but placing it last is conventional. Although optional, including it is good practice."},{"id":"switch-with-characters-and-enums","h":"Switch with characters and enums","text":"Output"},{"id":"rules-and-limitations","h":"Rules and limitations","text":"The controlling expression must have an integer type: int , char , short , long , or enum . Case labels must be constant integer expressions — no variables, no ranges, no strings. Labels must be unique within one switch. float , double and strings cannot be switched on. Declaring variables directly after a label needs a block { } ."},{"id":"switch-vs-if-else","h":"switch vs if...else","text":"Use switch when Use if...else when Comparing one value against several fixed constants Testing ranges ( x > 10 ) Working with an enum or menu choice Combining different variables The options are known at compile time Comparing strings or floating-point values"},{"id":"complete-example-menu","h":"Complete example: menu","text":"Output Warning: forgetting break after the last case can also cause trouble later when new cases are added. Some compilers warn about implicit fall-through; heed those warnings."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Write a switch that prints the name of a month from a number 1–12. Group the months by season using fall-through. Rewrite a simple calculator using switch on the operator character."}]},{"page":"c-loops.html","title":"C Loops","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-loops.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Loops","text":"C Tutorial › Loops C Loops Loops repeat a block of code. C has three loop statements — while , do...while and for — plus break and continue to control them."},{"id":"while-loop","h":"while loop","text":"Checks the condition before each iteration. If it is false at the start, the body never runs. Output Forgetting i++ makes the loop run forever."},{"id":"do-while-loop","h":"do...while loop","text":"Checks the condition after the body, so the body runs at least once. Note the semicolon after while (...) . Output"},{"id":"for-loop","h":"for loop","text":"The most common loop. It packs initialisation, condition and update into one line. Output Variations"},{"id":"which-loop-should-i-use","h":"Which loop should I use?","text":"Loop Use when for The number of iterations is known, or there is a clear counter. while Repeat while a condition holds; may run zero times. do...while The body must run at least once (menus, input validation)."},{"id":"nested-loops","h":"Nested loops","text":"A loop inside a loop. The inner loop completes fully for each iteration of the outer loop. Output Output"},{"id":"break","h":"break","text":"Leaves the innermost loop immediately. Execution continues after the loop. Output"},{"id":"continue","h":"continue","text":"Skips the rest of the current iteration and goes to the next one. Output"},{"id":"common-loop-patterns","h":"Common loop patterns","text":"Sum and average Output Factorial Output Reverse a number Output Input validation loop"},{"id":"common-mistakes","h":"Common mistakes","text":"Off-by-one — <= vs < . Choose deliberately. Forgetting to update the counter — infinite loop. Semicolon after the loop header — for (i = 0; i < 5; i++); creates an empty loop. Modifying the loop variable inside the body — can cause skipped or repeated iterations. Using the loop variable after the loop — in C99 its scope ends with the loop. break only exits one loop — nested loops need a flag or goto . Warning: an infinite loop is a logic bug, but some programs intentionally loop forever until break or user input. Make the exit condition obvious."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Print the multiplication table from 1 to 10. Print all prime numbers below 100. Read numbers until 0 is entered, then print their sum. Print a pyramid of stars with 6 rows. Count the digits in an integer using a loop."}]},{"page":"c-arrays.html","title":"C Arrays","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-arrays.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Arrays","text":"C Tutorial › Arrays C Arrays An array stores a fixed number of values of the same type in contiguous memory. Instead of ten separate variables, you get one name and an index."},{"id":"declaring-an-array","h":"Declaring an array","text":"The number in brackets is the number of elements , not the highest index. Indices run from 0 to size - 1 ."},{"id":"initialising-an-array","h":"Initialising an array","text":"Output"},{"id":"accessing-and-changing-elements","h":"Accessing and changing elements","text":"Use square brackets with an index. Indexing starts at zero. Output"},{"id":"looping-through-an-array","h":"Looping through an array","text":"Output"},{"id":"finding-the-size-safely","h":"Finding the size safely","text":"Use sizeof to compute the number of elements instead of repeating the size: This works only where the array itself is visible. Inside a function, an array parameter becomes a pointer and sizeof gives the pointer size — pass the length separately."},{"id":"out-of-bounds-access","h":"Out-of-bounds access","text":"Warning: C never checks array bounds. Reading or writing outside the array is undefined behaviour and a common source of security vulnerabilities. Always verify that 0 <= i < length ."},{"id":"two-dimensional-arrays","h":"Two-dimensional arrays","text":"A 2D array is an array of arrays — useful for grids, tables and matrices. Output"},{"id":"arrays-and-variables","h":"Arrays and variables","text":"An array name is not a modifiable variable; it acts like a pointer to the first element. You cannot assign one array to another with = ."},{"id":"passing-arrays-to-functions","h":"Passing arrays to functions","text":"Arrays decay to a pointer to their first element, so the function needs the length as a separate parameter. Output The const promises the function will not modify the caller's array."},{"id":"common-mistakes","h":"Common mistakes","text":"Starting at index 1 and using a[length] — both out of bounds. Forgetting { } in an initialiser and getting a compile error. Using sizeof inside a function on an array parameter. Declaring int a[0]; — not valid standard C. Assuming an array knows its own length — it does not."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Store 10 numbers in an array and print them in reverse. Find the largest and smallest element. Count how many elements are even. Create a 3×3 matrix and print its transpose. Write a function that searches an array for a value and returns its index (or −1)."}]},{"page":"c-strings.html","title":"C Strings","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-strings.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Strings","text":"C Tutorial › Strings C Strings C has no separate string type. A string is an array of char ending with a special null character '\\0' . Everything about strings follows from that one fact."},{"id":"string-literals-and-character-arrays","h":"String literals and character arrays","text":"Output"},{"id":"the-null-terminator","h":"The null terminator","text":"The array has five visible letters but six bytes. Functions such as printf keep reading until they find '\\0' . Without it, they run past the end of the array. Output"},{"id":"sizeof-vs-strlen","h":"sizeof vs strlen","text":"sizeof strlen When evaluated Compile time (for arrays) Run time Counts Allocated bytes, including '\\0' Characters before '\\0' Include header None <string.h> Result for \"Hello\" 6 5 On a pointer Size of the pointer (8) Length of the string"},{"id":"common-string-h-functions","h":"Common string.h functions","text":"Function Purpose strlen(s) Length, excluding '\\0' strcpy(dest, src) Copy a string (unsafe — no size limit) strncpy(dest, src, n) Copy at most n chars (may omit the terminator) strcat(dest, src) Append src to dest strncat(dest, src, n) Append at most n chars, then terminate strcmp(a, b) Compare; 0 if equal, <0 if a<b, >0 if a>b strncmp(a, b, n) Compare the first n characters strchr(s, c) Find first occurrence of a character strstr(s, sub) Find a substring memcpy(dest, src, n) Copy raw bytes (works for any type) memset(dest, value, n) Fill bytes with a value"},{"id":"copying-strings","h":"Copying strings","text":"Output You cannot assign strings with = after declaration. Use strcpy , or assign a new pointer."},{"id":"concatenating-strings","h":"Concatenating strings","text":"Output The destination must have enough space for the original text, the appended text and the terminator. Buffer overflows from unchecked strcat / strcpy are a classic security bug."},{"id":"comparing-strings","h":"Comparing strings","text":"== compares addresses, not contents. Use strcmp . Output"},{"id":"reading-strings","h":"Reading strings","text":"Prefer fgets over scanf(\"%s\", ...) : it accepts spaces and respects the buffer size. See User Input ."},{"id":"arrays-of-strings","h":"Arrays of strings","text":"Output"},{"id":"useful-character-functions-ctype-h","h":"Useful character functions (<ctype.h>)","text":"Function True when isalpha(c) Letter isdigit(c) Digit 0–9 isalnum(c) Letter or digit isspace(c) Space, tab, newline, etc. isupper(c) / islower(c) Upper / lower case toupper(c) / tolower(c) Convert case Output Tip: cast to unsigned char when calling <ctype.h> functions. Passing a negative char is undefined behaviour."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Count the vowels in a string. Reverse a string in place. Check whether a string is a palindrome. Convert a string to uppercase without using strupr . Write a function that counts occurrences of a character."}]},{"page":"c-input.html","title":"C User Input","group":"Tutorial","url":"c-input.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C User Input","text":"C Tutorial › User Input C User Input Programs read keyboard input from standard input ( stdin ). The two main tools are scanf for formatted values and fgets for lines of text."},{"id":"reading-a-number-with-scanf","h":"Reading a number with scanf","text":"scanf needs the address of the variable, so you write &age , not age . Input Output"},{"id":"scanf-conversion-specifiers","h":"scanf conversion specifiers","text":"Type Specifier Example int %d scanf(\"%d\", &n); unsigned %u scanf(\"%u\", &n); long %ld scanf(\"%ld\", &n); long long %lld scanf(\"%lld\", &n); float %f scanf(\"%f\", &f); double %lf scanf(\"%lf\", &d); char %c scanf(\" %c\", &c); (note the space) string %s scanf(\"%49s\", word); (no & ) Remember: use %f when printing a float or double with printf , but %lf when reading a double with scanf ."},{"id":"reading-several-values","h":"Reading several values","text":"Input Output"},{"id":"scanf-returns-a-count","h":"scanf returns a count","text":"The return value is the number of items successfully read, or EOF on failure. Always check it — otherwise bad input leaves the variable unchanged and your program misbehaves."},{"id":"reading-strings-safely-with-fgets","h":"Reading strings safely with fgets","text":"scanf(\"%s\") stops at whitespace and can overflow the buffer. fgets reads a whole line and takes a size limit. Input Output fgets returns NULL at end-of-file or on error. If the line is longer than the buffer, the rest stays in the input stream."},{"id":"reading-one-character-with-getchar","h":"Reading one character with getchar","text":"getchar returns an int so it can represent EOF (−1) as well as all byte values. Store its result in an int , not a char ."},{"id":"the-leftover-newline-problem","h":"The leftover-newline problem","text":"After scanf(\"%d\") , the newline stays in the input buffer. A following %c or fgets may read it immediately. The space in \" %c\" is not decoration: it tells scanf to skip whitespace before reading the character."},{"id":"parsing-a-whole-line-with-sscanf","h":"Parsing a whole line with sscanf","text":"Output"},{"id":"reading-command-line-arguments","h":"Reading command-line arguments","text":"Arguments passed after the program name arrive through main 's parameters. Command Output"},{"id":"input-checklist","h":"Input checklist","text":"Check the return value of scanf . Put & before numeric variables, but not before strings in scanf(\"%s\") . Use a width limit with %s : %49s for a 50-byte array. Use fgets for text that may contain spaces. Strip the newline left by fgets . Clear the input buffer when mixing scanf and character reads. Never use gets — it was removed from C11 because it cannot be made safe."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Read two numbers and print their sum, product and average. Read a name with spaces using fgets and greet the user. Ask for an age repeatedly until a valid positive number is entered. Read a line like \"item 3 19.99\" with sscanf and print a formatted receipt line."}]},{"page":"c-functions.html","title":"C Functions","group":"Advanced","url":"c-functions.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Functions","text":"C Tutorial › Functions C Functions A function is a named block of code that performs a task. Functions let you reuse logic, keep main short, and test pieces of a program independently."},{"id":"defining-and-calling-a-function","h":"Defining and calling a function","text":"Output void as the return type means the function returns no value. (void) means it takes no parameters. A function must be declared before it is called — either define it above the call or write a prototype."},{"id":"parameters-and-return-values","h":"Parameters and return values","text":"Output"},{"id":"function-prototypes","h":"Function prototypes","text":"A prototype tells the compiler the function's signature before its body appears. It lets you put main first and helper functions below. Output"},{"id":"pass-by-value","h":"Pass by value","text":"C passes arguments by value : the function receives copies. Changing a parameter does not change the caller's variable. Output To let a function modify the caller's variable, pass its address and accept a pointer — see the Pointers chapter ."},{"id":"returning-values","h":"Returning values","text":"Return at most one value in C. To return several results, use pointers as output parameters or return a structure."},{"id":"local-scope","h":"Local scope","text":"Variables declared inside a function are local and disappear when it returns. Each call gets a fresh copy. Use static to keep a local variable alive between calls: Output"},{"id":"recursion","h":"Recursion","text":"A recursive function calls itself. Every recursive function needs a base case that stops the recursion. Output Warning: each recursive call uses stack memory. Deep recursion can overflow the stack and crash. Prefer iteration when the depth may be large."},{"id":"functions-and-arrays","h":"Functions and arrays","text":"Output"},{"id":"multiple-source-files","h":"Multiple source files","text":"Large programs are split across files. Put declarations in a header and definitions in a .c file. Compile all the source files together: gcc main.c math_utils.c -o app ."},{"id":"good-function-design","h":"Good function design","text":"One job per function. If you need \"and\" to describe it, split it. Short parameter lists. More than four or five parameters suggests a structure. Clear names. calculate_average beats calc . Document behaviour — especially edge cases and error returns. Avoid hidden state. Prefer parameters and return values over globals."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Write is_prime(int n) that returns 1 or 0. Write a function that reverses an array in place. Write recursive and iterative versions of a power function. Write swap(int *a, int *b) using pointers. Split a small program into a .c file and a .h header."}]},{"page":"c-pointers.html","title":"C Pointers","group":"Advanced","url":"c-pointers.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Pointers","text":"C Tutorial › Pointers C Pointers A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another value. Pointers are what make C powerful — and what make it dangerous."},{"id":"addresses-and-the-operator","h":"Addresses and the & operator","text":"Every variable lives at some address. The unary & operator (\"address of\") gives you that address. Output The exact address changes on every run. Addresses are printed with %p ."},{"id":"declaring-a-pointer","h":"Declaring a pointer","text":"The * in a declaration means \"pointer to\". The type matters: it tells the compiler how many bytes to read when dereferencing and how far to step in pointer arithmetic."},{"id":"dereferencing-with","h":"Dereferencing with *","text":"The unary * operator (\"dereference\") accesses the value at the address. Output Changing *p changes value because both refer to the same memory."},{"id":"null-pointers","h":"NULL pointers","text":"A pointer that does not point anywhere should be set to NULL (defined in <stddef.h> ). Check before dereferencing. Output Never dereference NULL. *p when p == NULL usually crashes the program. It is also good practice to set a pointer to NULL after freeing its memory."},{"id":"pointers-and-functions","h":"Pointers and functions","text":"Pass an address to let a function modify the caller's variable. This is how scanf works. Output Note (*n)++ : the parentheses are required. *n++ means \"increment the pointer, then dereference\"."},{"id":"pointers-and-arrays","h":"Pointers and arrays","text":"An array name decays to a pointer to its first element. a[i] is defined as *(a + i) . Output"},{"id":"pointer-arithmetic","h":"Pointer arithmetic","text":"Adding 1 to a pointer moves it to the next element — not the next byte. The step size is the size of the pointed-to type. Subtracting two pointers into the same array gives the number of elements between them. Adding two pointers is meaningless and not allowed."},{"id":"pointers-to-structures","h":"Pointers to structures","text":"Use -> to access a member through a pointer. It is shorthand for (*ptr).member . Output"},{"id":"function-pointers","h":"Function pointers","text":"Functions also have addresses. A function pointer can choose behaviour at run time. Output"},{"id":"common-pointer-bugs","h":"Common pointer bugs","text":"Uninitialised pointer — points at a random address. Always initialise to NULL or a real address. Dangling pointer — points at memory that has been freed or gone out of scope. Double free — calling free twice on the same allocation. Wild write — *p = 5 when p was never valid. Silent memory corruption. Pointer type mismatch — reading a double through an int * gives nonsense. Forgetting & — passing an int where an int * is expected. Tip: compile with -Wall -Wextra and run memory-sensitive code under Valgrind (Linux) or AddressSanitizer ( gcc -fsanitize=address ). They catch the bugs that \"seem to work\"."},{"id":"a-mental-model","h":"A mental model","text":"Think of memory as a street of numbered houses. A variable is a house containing a value. A pointer is a piece of paper with a house number written on it. Dereferencing means walking to that house and reading or writing what is inside."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Declare an int, print its address, and store the address in a pointer. Write a function that swaps two integers using pointers. Use a pointer to find the largest element of an array. Write a function that returns the length of a string using pointer arithmetic. Explain the difference between *p++ and (*p)++ ."}]},{"page":"c-structures.html","title":"C Structures","group":"Advanced","url":"c-structures.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Structures","text":"C Tutorial › Structures C Structures A structure ( struct ) groups variables of different types under one name. It is how C models real-world objects: a point, a student, a bank account."},{"id":"defining-a-structure","h":"Defining a structure","text":"This defines a new type, struct Point , with two members. It does not create any variable yet. Note the semicolon after the closing brace."},{"id":"creating-and-using-a-structure","h":"Creating and using a structure","text":"Output Access members with the dot operator . ."},{"id":"initialising-structures","h":"Initialising structures","text":"Output"},{"id":"a-realistic-example","h":"A realistic example","text":"Output"},{"id":"pointers-to-structures","h":"Pointers to structures","text":"Use -> instead of (*ptr). : Output"},{"id":"arrays-of-structures","h":"Arrays of structures","text":"Output"},{"id":"nested-structures","h":"Nested structures","text":"Output"},{"id":"structures-and-functions","h":"Structures and functions","text":"Passing a structure by value copies all its members. For large structures, pass a pointer instead — preferably const if the function only reads. Output"},{"id":"typedef-shorter-names","h":"typedef: shorter names","text":"typedef creates an alias, so you can write Point instead of struct Point . You can also define the struct and the alias together, as above. The structure tag is then optional."},{"id":"self-referential-structures","h":"Self-referential structures","text":"A structure can contain a pointer to another structure of the same type — the basis of linked lists and trees. Output"},{"id":"size-padding-and-alignment","h":"Size, padding and alignment","text":"Structures may contain padding bytes so members align on sensible boundaries. As a result, the size of a struct can be larger than the sum of its members. Output Order members from largest to smallest to reduce padding. Use #pragma pack only when a binary format requires it."},{"id":"common-mistakes","h":"Common mistakes","text":"Forgetting the semicolon after the closing brace of a struct definition. Using == to compare structures — C does not define it. Compare members or use memcmp (careful with padding). Assigning to a string member with = — use strcpy . Using . on a pointer or -> on a value. Assuming sizeof(struct) equals the sum of member sizes."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Define a Book structure with title, author, year and price. Print one book. Create an array of five books and print the most expensive. Write a function that takes a pointer to a Book and applies a 10% discount. Define a nested Date inside an Event structure and print it. Build a three-node linked list and traverse it."}]},{"page":"c-unions-enums.html","title":"C Unions & Enums","group":"Advanced","url":"c-unions-enums.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Unions & Enums","text":"C Tutorial › Unions & Enums C Unions & Enums Unions and enumerations are two small but useful tools: a union lets several members share one block of memory, and an enum gives names to a fixed set of integers."},{"id":"unions","h":"Unions","text":"A union looks like a structure, but all members share the same storage . Its size is the size of its largest member. Writing one member overwrites the others. Output"},{"id":"union-vs-struct","h":"Union vs struct","text":"struct union Memory Each member has its own space All members overlap Size Sum of members + padding Size of largest member Simultaneous values Yes No — only the last written member is valid Typical use Objects with several fields One value that can have different types"},{"id":"tagged-unions","h":"Tagged unions","text":"Because a union does not remember which member is active, pair it with an enum tag inside a structure. Output"},{"id":"enumerations","h":"Enumerations","text":"An enum declares a set of named integer constants. It makes code readable and restricts values to a known list. Output"},{"id":"custom-enum-values","h":"Custom enum values","text":"When you assign one value, the following names continue counting from it:"},{"id":"typedef-enum","h":"typedef enum","text":"The tag is optional, but when you use one you normally also create a typedef for convenience. Output"},{"id":"enum-with-a-switch","h":"Enum with a switch","text":"Output"},{"id":"enum-vs-define","h":"Enum vs #define","text":"enum #define Type-safe Yes (an enum type) No Debugger visible Often yes No Scope Block scope Preprocessor, file scope Values Integer constants only Any token sequence Best for Related named options Configuration, include guards, macros"},{"id":"practical-notes","h":"Practical notes","text":"An enum's underlying type is an implementation-defined integer type large enough to hold all values. Enumerators are visible in the surrounding scope (unlike struct members). C does not prevent assigning an integer to an enum variable — the type is a helpful convention, not a strict guarantee. Use enums for states, options, error codes and command types. Do not take the address of a union member you did not last write — reading an inactive member is implementation-defined and often meaningless. Warning: type-punning through unions (writing one member and reading another) is a common but non-portable trick. Unless your compiler documents it, avoid relying on it. Use memcpy for safe reinterpretation."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Define an enum for the four seasons and print one. Create a tagged union that stores either an int or a string, plus a tag. Use enum flags to represent read/write/execute permissions and test whether a set of permissions includes write access. Write a function that returns a season name from an enum value."}]},{"page":"c-files.html","title":"C File Handling","group":"Advanced","url":"c-files.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C File Handling","text":"C Tutorial › File Handling C File Handling Files let a program keep data after it exits. C uses a FILE * stream for both text and binary files, with functions from <stdio.h> ."},{"id":"the-four-steps","h":"The four steps","text":"Open the file with fopen . Check that the returned pointer is not NULL . Read or write using the stream functions. Close the file with fclose ."},{"id":"opening-files-fopen-modes","h":"Opening files: fopen modes","text":"Mode Meaning If the file exists If not \"r\" Read text Opens Fails ( NULL ) \"w\" Write text Truncates Creates \"a\" Append text Keeps content, writes at end Creates \"r+\" Read and write Opens Fails \"w+\" Read and write Truncates Creates \"a+\" Read and append Keeps content Creates \"rb\" , \"wb\" , \"ab\" Binary versions Same rules, no newline translation Warning: \"w\" erases the file immediately. Use \"a\" if you want to keep existing content."},{"id":"writing-a-text-file","h":"Writing a text file","text":"Output"},{"id":"reading-a-text-file","h":"Reading a text file","text":"Line by line with fgets Output Formatted reading with fscanf fscanf returns the number of items read, or EOF when input runs out. Always check it."},{"id":"binary-files","h":"Binary files","text":"Text functions stop at whitespace and translate newlines. For raw bytes — images, structs, arrays — use fread and fwrite . Output Binary files are not portable across systems with different type sizes or endianness. For data exchange, prefer a text format such as CSV or JSON."},{"id":"moving-around-fseek-and-ftell","h":"Moving around: fseek and ftell","text":"Function Purpose fseek(fp, offset, origin) Move the position indicator ftell(fp) Current position in bytes rewind(fp) Go back to the start feof(fp) Non-zero after an attempted read past the end ferror(fp) Non-zero if an error occurred clearerr(fp) Clear EOF and error flags Origins: SEEK_SET (start), SEEK_CUR (current), SEEK_END (end). Output"},{"id":"error-handling","h":"Error handling","text":"perror prints your message plus the system's reason, using errno . Output"},{"id":"reading-and-writing-at-the-same-time","h":"Reading and writing at the same time","text":"When a stream is opened for both reading and writing, you must call fflush or a positioning function between a write and a read (and vice versa). Forgetting this is undefined behaviour."},{"id":"closing-files","h":"Closing files","text":"fclose flushes buffered output and releases the stream. If you forget, data may be lost when the program exits abnormally, and you may run out of file handles."},{"id":"standard-streams","h":"Standard streams","text":"Stream Purpose Example stdin Keyboard / redirected input fgets(line, n, stdin) stdout Normal output printf(...) stderr Error output (unbuffered) fprintf(stderr, ...) Tip: always check fopen , fread , fwrite and fclose . Disk-full, permission and missing-file errors are normal, not exceptional."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Write a program that saves five names to names.txt . Read the file back and print each name with its line number. Append a timestamp line to a log file. Copy one file to another using fgetc / fputc . Count the lines, words and characters in a text file."}]},{"page":"c-memory.html","title":"C Memory","group":"Advanced","url":"c-memory.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Memory","text":"C Tutorial › Memory C Memory Management C does not manage memory for you. You decide when to allocate it and when to release it. Understanding the stack and the heap is essential for writing correct C programs."},{"id":"stack-vs-heap","h":"Stack vs heap","text":"Stack Heap Allocation Automatic (local variables) Manual ( malloc family) Lifetime Until the function returns Until you call free Size Small (often 1–8 MB total) Limited by available RAM Speed Very fast Slower, involves the allocator Flexibility Size known at compile time (or VLA) Size can be decided at run time"},{"id":"malloc","h":"malloc","text":"malloc(size) reserves size bytes and returns a void * to the block, or NULL if it fails. The memory is not initialised . Output sizeof *values is better than sizeof(int) : it stays correct if the type of values changes."},{"id":"calloc","h":"calloc","text":"calloc(count, size) allocates space for an array and zeroes it . Output"},{"id":"realloc","h":"realloc","text":"realloc(ptr, new_size) resizes an existing block. It may move it, so always assign the result to the same pointer and check for NULL . Output Warning: do not write values = realloc(values, ...) directly. If realloc fails, you lose the original pointer and leak the memory."},{"id":"free","h":"free","text":"free(ptr) releases memory obtained from malloc , calloc or realloc . After freeing, set the pointer to NULL to avoid accidental reuse."},{"id":"common-memory-bugs","h":"Common memory bugs","text":"Bug What happens Avoid by Memory leak Allocated block is never freed; memory grows Free every successful allocation Use after free Reading/writing freed memory Set pointer to NULL ; restructure ownership Double free Freeing the same pointer twice Free once; null the pointer Buffer overflow Writing past the allocation Check indices and lengths Off-by-one Allocating n but using n+1 Use count * sizeof consistently Unchecked NULL Dereferencing a failed allocation Check every allocation Freeing stack memory Passing a non-heap pointer to free Only free what you allocated"},{"id":"ownership","h":"Ownership","text":"For every allocation, decide who owns it and when it is freed . A simple convention: the function that allocates returns ownership to the caller, and the caller frees it. Document this in the function's comment."},{"id":"when-not-to-use-malloc","h":"When not to use malloc","text":"For small, fixed-size data — use a local array or a struct. When the lifetime is exactly one function call — use the stack. In tight loops — allocator calls are relatively expensive. When a static buffer is enough and the program is single-threaded. Tip: pair every malloc / calloc / realloc with exactly one free on every path, including error paths. A short checklist prevents most leaks."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Allocate an array of 10 ints, fill it with squares, print it, then free it. Write a function that duplicates a string on the heap. Grow a dynamic array from 2 to 8 elements with realloc . Create a linked list of three nodes with malloc and free every node. Run a deliberately leaky program under Valgrind or AddressSanitizer and read the report."}]},{"page":"c-preprocessor.html","title":"C Preprocessor","group":"Advanced","url":"c-preprocessor.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Preprocessor","text":"C Tutorial › Preprocessor C Preprocessor Before compilation, the preprocessor scans the source for lines beginning with # and transforms the text. It knows nothing about C types or syntax — it only substitutes and includes."},{"id":"include","h":"#include","text":"Copies the contents of another file into your source at that point. Form Searches Use for <name.h> System include directories Standard and installed libraries \"name.h\" Current directory, then system directories Your own project headers"},{"id":"define-object-like-macros","h":"#define: object-like macros","text":"Output The preprocessor performs text substitution . PI is replaced everywhere before the compiler runs."},{"id":"define-function-like-macros","h":"#define: function-like macros","text":"Output Parenthesise everything. #define SQUARE(x) x * x makes SQUARE(2 + 3) expand to 2 + 3 * 2 + 3 = 11. Even with parentheses, arguments may be evaluated twice — prefer static inline functions for real logic."},{"id":"conditional-compilation","h":"Conditional compilation","text":"Compile different code depending on macros. This is how one codebase supports several platforms. Output"},{"id":"ifdef-and-ifndef","h":"#ifdef and #ifndef","text":"Directive Meaning #if expr Compile the block if expr is non-zero #ifdef NAME Compile if NAME is defined #ifndef NAME Compile if NAME is not defined #elif expr Else-if for the preprocessor #else Fallback branch #endif Ends the conditional block"},{"id":"include-guards","h":"Include guards","text":"If a header is included twice, its definitions appear twice and the compiler complains. Include guards prevent that. Many compilers also support the shorter #pragma once , but include guards are standard C."},{"id":"predefined-macros","h":"Predefined macros","text":"Macro Meaning __FILE__ Current source file name __LINE__ Current line number __DATE__ Compilation date __TIME__ Compilation time __func__ Current function name (C99, not a macro strictly) __STDC_VERSION__ Standard version, e.g. 201710L Output"},{"id":"preprocessor-pitfalls","h":"Preprocessor pitfalls","text":"No type checking — macros accept anything. Side effects — arguments can be evaluated more than once. Operator precedence — missing parentheses change meaning. Debugging — macro-expanded code is harder to step through. Name clashes — macros ignore scope and can replace unintended identifiers. Semicolons — never end a macro definition with ; unless you want it in every expansion."},{"id":"viewing-preprocessor-output","h":"Viewing preprocessor output","text":"Reading the preprocessed output is the fastest way to understand why a macro is misbehaving. Tip: use the preprocessor for inclusion, platform switches and simple constants; use const , enum and static inline functions for everything the type system can check."},{"id":"exercises","h":"Exercises","text":"Write a header file with an include guard and include it twice in one file. Create a SQUARE macro and verify it works with expressions. Use #ifdef to print different messages on Windows and Linux. Write a LOG macro that prints the file, line and message. Run gcc -E on a small file and inspect the expansion."}]},{"page":"c-exercises.html","title":"C Exercises","group":"Practice","url":"c-exercises.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Exercises","text":"C Tutorial › Practice › Exercises C Exercises Reading C is not the same as writing C. These exercises go from printing a line to pointers, structures and dynamic memory. Try each one before opening the solution — then press Try it on a solution to run it in the offline editor. 0 of 18 solved How to use this page. Write your attempt in the Try It Editor , or click Try it on any solution. Tick the checkbox when you have solved an exercise — ticks are saved in this browser. The editor uses PicoC, a C interpreter, so a few advanced programs are best tested with GCC or Clang."},{"id":"beginner","h":"Beginner","text":"1. Print a greeting Easy Ask for a first name with scanf , then print Hello, <name>! on one line. Related: C Output · C User Input Show solution Output 2. Add two numbers Easy Read two integers and print their sum, difference, product and integer quotient on separate lines. Related: C Variables · C Operators Show solution 3. Even or odd Easy Read an integer and report whether it is even or odd. Use the remainder operator. Related: C If...Else Show solution 4. Grade calculator Easy Convert a score from 0–100 to a letter grade: A ≥ 90, B ≥ 80, C ≥ 70, D ≥ 60, otherwise F. Reject scores outside 0–100. Related: C If...Else Show solution 5. Multiplication table Easy Print the multiplication table for a number from 1 to 10 using a for loop, formatted as 7 x 3 = 21 . Related: C Loops Show solution 6. Countdown Easy Read a positive number and count down to zero with a while loop, then print Liftoff! . Related: C Loops Show solution 7. Largest of three Easy Read three integers and print the largest. Do it once with if statements and once with the conditional operator ?: . Related: C Operators · C If...Else Show solution 8. Simple calculator Easy Read two numbers and an operator ( + - * / ) "},{"id":"intermediate","h":"Intermediate","text":"9. Array statistics Medium Read 10 integers into an array and print the sum, average (as a double ), minimum and maximum. Related: C Arrays Show solution 10. Reverse a string Medium Read a word and print it backwards. Write your own loop first; then compare with a version using strlen . Related: C Strings Show solution 11. Count vowels Medium Count the vowels (a, e, i, o, u) in a sentence read with fgets . Treat uppercase letters as vowels too. Related: C Strings · C User Input Show solution 12. Palindrome check Medium Read a word and decide whether it reads the same forwards and backwards. Ignore case. Related: C Strings Show solution 13. Factorial and Fibonacci Medium Write two functions: one returns n! with a loop, the other returns the nth Fibonacci number with recursion. Print the first 10 values of each. Related: C Functions Show solution 14. Swap with pointers Medium Write void swap(int *a, int *b) and prove that the caller's variables change. Related: C Pointers Show solution 15. Bubble sort Medium Sort an array of 8 integers in ascending order using bubble sort, then print the result. Related: C Arrays · C Loops Show solution 16. Student record Medium Define a struct Stude"},{"id":"advanced","h":"Advanced","text":"17. Dynamic array Hard Ask for a count, allocate an int array with malloc , fill it, print it, then free it. Check that allocation succeeded. Related: C Memory Show solution 18. Prime sieve Hard Print every prime below 100 using the Sieve of Eratosthenes. Use an array of flags and a loop that starts at i * i . Related: C Arrays · C Loops Show solution Finished all 18? Take the 15-question quiz , then generate your course certificate . If you want more practice, rebuild each program from memory the next day."}]},{"page":"c-quiz.html","title":"C Quiz","group":"Practice","url":"c-quiz.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Quiz","text":"C Tutorial › Quiz C Quiz Fifteen questions covering the whole tutorial. Pick an answer for each, then check your score. Explanations appear after checking. 1. Which header declares printf ? <stdlib.h> <stdio.h> <string.h> <math.h> B. printf is part of the standard input/output library in <stdio.h> . 2. Which is a valid definition of the entry point? void main() main() int main(void) int main[] C. The standard form returns int . void main is not standard C. 3. What does printf(\"%d\", 7 / 2); print? 3 3.5 4 7 A. Integer division truncates the fractional part. 4. Which condition tests whether x equals 5? if (x = 5) if (x == 5) if (x === 5) if (x != 5) B. == compares; = assigns. === does not exist in C. 5. What is the first valid index of an array? 0 1 -1 It depends on the compiler A. C arrays are zero-indexed: valid indices are 0 to length - 1 . 6. Which loop always executes its body at least once? while for do...while None of them C. do...while checks its condition after running the body. 7. What character marks the end of a C string? '\\n' '\\0' EOF 'NULL' B. A null character '\\0' (value 0) terminates every C string. 8. How many bytes does char s[] = \"Hi\"; occupy? 2 3 4 8 B. Two visibl"},{"id":"where-to-review","h":"Where to review","text":"Data types Types, sizes and format specifiers. Arrays Indexing and bounds. Strings Null terminators and string.h. Pointers Addresses, dereferencing and NULL. Memory malloc and free. File handling fopen modes and error checks."}]},{"page":"c-certificate.html","title":"C Certificate","group":"Practice","url":"c-certificate.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Certificate","text":"C Tutorial › Practice › Certificate Course Certificate Finish the tutorial and pass the quiz, then print a certificate with your name. Everything is calculated and stored in your browser — there is no sign-up and nothing is uploaded."},{"id":"requirements","h":"Requirements","text":"✓ Complete every tutorial chapter — 0 of 22 done. ✓ Score at least 70% on the C quiz . Best saved score: not taken yet ."},{"id":"your-name","h":"Your name","text":"Name as it should appear on the certificate Generate certificate Print / Save as PDF Reset progress C Notes · Offline Course"},{"id":"certificate-of-completion","h":"Certificate of Completion","text":"This certifies that Your Name has completed the C Notes offline course. Date Course C Programming Fundamentals Format Self-paced · Offline Certified This is a personal record of study generated by this website. It is not an accredited qualification and has no monetary value. How progress is measured. Each tutorial page has a Mark as complete button. The quiz saves your best score automatically. Clearing your browser’s site data will erase this progress, so print or save the certificate as a PDF when you are done."}]},{"page":"c-reference.html","title":"C Reference","group":"Reference","url":"c-reference.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Reference","text":"C Tutorial › Reference C Reference A quick lookup for the things you reach for while writing C: keywords, types, format specifiers, headers and library functions."},{"id":"keywords","h":"Keywords","text":"C89/C90 has 32 keywords. C99 added five, C11 added seven, and C23 added several more. Category Keywords Types char double float int long short signed unsigned void _Bool Storage / qualifiers auto const extern register static volatile inline restrict _Atomic _Thread_local Control flow break case continue default do else for goto if return switch while Aggregate types enum struct union typedef Operators sizeof _Alignof _Generic Other _Static_assert _Noreturn _Complex _Imaginary All keywords are lowercase and reserved. You cannot use them as identifiers."},{"id":"data-types","h":"Data types","text":"Type Typical size Typical range printf scanf char 1 −128 to 127 %c %c unsigned char 1 0 to 255 %hhu %hhu short 2 −32,768 to 32,767 %hd %hd unsigned short 2 0 to 65,535 %hu %hu int 4 −2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647 %d %d unsigned int 4 0 to 4,294,967,295 %u %u long 4 or 8 platform dependent %ld %ld unsigned long 4 or 8 platform dependent %lu %lu long long 8 ±9.2 × 10 18 %lld %lld float 4 ~7 digits precision %f %f double 8 ~15 digits precision %f / %lf %lf long double 8–16 extended precision %Lf %Lf size_t 4 or 8 unsigned, holds sizes %zu %zu char * 4 or 8 string %s %s void * 4 or 8 any address %p —"},{"id":"format-specifiers","h":"Format specifiers","text":"Specifier Meaning Example %d %i Signed decimal int -42 %u Unsigned decimal 42 %o Octal 52 %x %X Hexadecimal 2a / 2A %f Fixed-point decimal 3.140000 %e %E Scientific notation 3.140000e+00 %g %G Shorter of %f and %e 3.14 %c Single character A %s String Hello %p Pointer 0x7ffd... %% Literal percent % %n Store characters written so far Rarely used; avoid Flag / width Effect Example %5d Minimum width 5, right aligned 42 %-5d Left aligned 42 %05d Zero padded 00042 %+d Always show sign +42 %.2f Two decimal places 3.14 %8.2f Width 8, two decimals 3.14 %*d Width from an argument printf(\"%*d\", 5, 42); %.*f Precision from an argument printf(\"%.*f\", 2, 3.14159);"},{"id":"escape-sequences","h":"Escape sequences","text":"Sequence Meaning ASCII \\n Newline 10 \\t Horizontal tab 9 \\r Carriage return 13 \\b Backspace 8 \\f Form feed 12 \\v Vertical tab 11 \\a Alert (bell) 7 \\\\ Backslash 92 \\' Single quote 39 \\\" Double quote 34 \\? Question mark 63 \\0 Null 0 \\xHH Hex character code varies \\ooo Octal character code varies"},{"id":"standard-headers","h":"Standard headers","text":"Header Provides <stdio.h> Input/output: printf, scanf, FILE, fopen <stdlib.h> malloc, free, atoi, rand, exit, qsort <string.h> strlen, strcpy, strcmp, memcpy, memset <math.h> sqrt, pow, sin, cos, floor, ceil, fabs <ctype.h> isalpha, isdigit, toupper, tolower <stdbool.h> bool, true, false (C99) <stdint.h> int8_t, uint32_t, INT_MAX, SIZE_MAX (C99) <inttypes.h> PRId32, PRIu64 format macros <limits.h> INT_MAX, CHAR_MIN, LONG_MAX <float.h> FLT_MAX, DBL_EPSILON, DBL_DIG <stddef.h> size_t, ptrdiff_t, NULL, offsetof <time.h> time, clock, strftime, struct tm <assert.h> assert macro <errno.h> errno, ENOENT, EINVAL <stdarg.h> va_list, va_start, va_arg, va_end <signal.h> signal, raise, SIGINT <locale.h> setlocale, LC_ALL <setjmp.h> setjmp, longjmp <stdatomic.h> Atomic types (C11) <threads.h> Threads (C11, optional)"},{"id":"stdio-h-functions","h":"<stdio.h> functions","text":"Function Purpose printf , fprintf , sprintf , snprintf Formatted output scanf , fscanf , sscanf Formatted input puts , fputs Write a string fgets Read a line safely getchar , putchar Read/write one character fgetc , fputc Read/write one character from a stream fopen , fclose Open/close a file fread , fwrite Binary read/write fseek , ftell , rewind File positioning fflush Flush buffered output feof , ferror , clearerr Stream status perror Print an error message remove , rename Delete or rename a file"},{"id":"stdlib-h-functions","h":"<stdlib.h> functions","text":"Function Purpose malloc , calloc , realloc , free Dynamic memory exit , abort , atexit Program termination atoi , atol , atof String to number (no error reporting) strtol , strtoul , strtod String to number with error reporting rand , srand Pseudo-random numbers qsort , bsearch Sorting and searching abs , labs Absolute value getenv Read an environment variable system Run a shell command"},{"id":"string-h-functions","h":"<string.h> functions","text":"Function Purpose strlen Length, excluding the null terminator strcpy , strncpy Copy strings strcat , strncat Concatenate strings strcmp , strncmp Compare strings strchr , strrchr Find a character strstr Find a substring strtok Split into tokens (modifies the input) memcpy Copy bytes (no overlap) memmove Copy bytes (handles overlap) memcmp Compare bytes memset Fill bytes"},{"id":"math-h-functions","h":"<math.h> functions","text":"Function Purpose sqrt(x) , cbrt(x) Square root, cube root pow(x, y) x raised to y fabs(x) Absolute value (double) floor(x) , ceil(x) , round(x) Rounding fmod(x, y) Floating-point remainder sin , cos , tan Trigonometry (radians) log , log10 , exp Logarithms and exponential fmin , fmax Minimum / maximum isnan , isinf Test for NaN / infinity On Linux, link with -lm : gcc main.c -lm -o main ."},{"id":"compiler-quick-reference","h":"Compiler quick reference","text":"Task GCC / Clang MSVC Compile gcc main.c -o main cl main.c Warnings -Wall -Wextra /W4 Standard -std=c17 /std:c17 Debug info -g /Zi Optimise -O2 /O2 Preprocess only -E /P Assembly output -S /FA Math library -lm (linked by default)"},{"id":"frequently-seen-errors","h":"Frequently seen errors","text":"Message Usual cause expected ';' before ... Missing semicolon on the previous line undeclared identifier Typo, missing declaration, or missing #include implicit declaration of function Missing header or prototype undefined reference to ... Function not linked; forgot to compile a source file or -lm segmentation fault Invalid pointer, out-of-bounds access, NULL dereference format '%d' expects int Wrong printf specifier for the argument type control reaches end of non-void function Missing return on some path conflicting types for ... Prototype and definition disagree Tip: bookmark this page. It is designed to be scanned, not read start to finish."}]},{"page":"c-cheatsheet.html","title":"C Cheat Sheet","group":"Reference","url":"c-cheatsheet.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Cheat Sheet","text":"C Tutorial › Reference › Cheat Sheet C Cheat Sheet The whole language on one page: syntax, types, operators, pointers, memory, files and the functions you will actually use. Keep it open while you practise — or print it. 🖨 Print this cheat sheet"},{"id":"skeleton","h":"Program skeleton","text":"Compile: gcc main.c -o main · Run: ./main (Windows: main.exe ). Add -Wall -Wextra -std=c17 for useful warnings."},{"id":"types","h":"Data types","text":"Type Typical size Use / range Format char 1 byte one character / small integer; −128…127 %c unsigned char 1 byte 0…255 (bytes, buffers) %hhu short 2 bytes at least ±32,767 %hd int 4 bytes usually ±2.1 billion %d unsigned int 4 bytes 0…4,294,967,295 %u long 4 or 8 bytes at least 32 bits %ld long long 8 bytes at least 64 bits %lld float 4 bytes ~6 decimal digits %f double 8 bytes ~15 decimal digits (default) %lf / %f size_t 8 bytes sizes, indexes; unsigned %zu void — no value / generic pointer — Use sizeof(type) for the real size on your machine. <stdint.h> gives exact types: int8_t , uint32_t , int64_t ."},{"id":"format","h":"printf and scanf formats","text":"Specifier Output (printf) Input (scanf) %d / %i signed int signed int %u unsigned int unsigned int %f double (6 decimals by default) float %lf double double %c one character one character (does not skip space) %s string up to \\0 word up to whitespace %x / %X hexadecimal hexadecimal %o octal octal %p pointer address pointer address %% a literal % — Width and precision: %8.2f (8 wide, 2 decimals), %-8d (left aligned), %08d (zero padded), %.3s (first 3 characters). For input, limit strings: scanf(\"%63s\", name) ."},{"id":"escapes","h":"Escape sequences","text":"\\n newline \\t tab \\\\ backslash \\\" double quote \\' single quote \\0 null terminator \\a bell \\r carriage return \\b backspace \\f form feed \\v vertical tab \\x41 hex character"},{"id":"operators","h":"Operators and precedence","text":"Priority Operators Notes 1 () [] . -> ++ -- postfix forms, member access 2 ! ~ ++ -- + - * & (type) sizeof unary / cast / dereference / address 3 * / % multiplicative 4 + - additive 5 << >> bit shifts 6 < <= > >= relational 7 == != equality 8–10 & ^ | bitwise AND, XOR, OR 11–12 && || logical, short-circuit 13 ?: conditional 14 = += -= *= /= %= &= |= ^= <<= >>= assignment, right to left 15 , comma"},{"id":"control","h":"Control flow","text":"if / else switch Loops"},{"id":"functions","h":"Functions and storage","text":"Keyword Meaning static At file scope: visible only in this file. Inside a function: keeps its value between calls. extern Declares something defined in another file. const Value cannot be changed through this name. inline Hint to expand the function at the call site. register Historic hint; rarely useful today. volatile The value may change outside the program's control (hardware, signals). Prevents some optimisations."},{"id":"pointers","h":"Pointers and arrays","text":"a[i] is exactly *(a + i) . Passing an array to a function passes a pointer; the function cannot know its length unless you pass it. NULL means “points to nothing”. Always check a pointer before dereferencing it. Pointer arithmetic moves in units of the pointed-to type: p + 1 skips sizeof(*p) bytes."},{"id":"memory","h":"Memory management","text":"Function Purpose malloc(size) Allocate uninitialised bytes; returns NULL on failure. calloc(n, size) Allocate n zeroed elements. realloc(p, size) Resize an allocation; may move it. free(p) Release memory; p must come from malloc/calloc/realloc. Every malloc needs exactly one matching free . Never free the same block twice, and never use memory after freeing it."},{"id":"files","h":"File I/O","text":"Mode Meaning \"r\" Read; fails if the file does not exist. \"w\" Write; creates or truncates. \"a\" Append; creates if needed. \"r+\" / \"w+\" / \"a+\" Read and write variants. \"rb\" / \"wb\" Binary mode (important on Windows)."},{"id":"library","h":"Standard library quick list","text":"Header Common functions <stdio.h> printf , scanf , puts , fgets , fopen , fclose , fprintf , fread , fwrite <stdlib.h> malloc , calloc , realloc , free , atoi , strtol , rand , qsort , exit <string.h> strlen , strcpy , strncpy , strcmp , strcat , strchr , strstr , memcpy , memset <math.h> sqrt , pow , fabs , floor , ceil , round , sin , cos , log <ctype.h> isalpha , isdigit , isspace , isupper , tolower , toupper <time.h> time , clock , difftime , strftime <stdbool.h> bool , true , false <stdint.h> int8_t , uint32_t , int64_t , INT_MAX <limits.h> INT_MAX , INT_MIN , CHAR_BIT , LONG_MAX <assert.h> assert(condition) — aborts if false"},{"id":"errors","h":"Common beginner errors","text":"Symptom Likely cause expected ';' before ... Missing semicolon, brace or parenthesis on the previous line. undefined reference to 'foo' Function declared but not defined, or the object file/library was not linked. implicit declaration of function Missing #include or prototype. Program crashes with a segmentation fault Dereferencing NULL/uninitialised pointer, out-of-bounds array access, or using freed memory. scanf seems to skip input Use \" %c\" (leading space) after reading a number, or read whole lines with fgets . Infinite loop Loop variable never changes, or the condition can never become false. Garbage string output Missing '\\0' , buffer overflow, or returning a pointer to a local array. Wrong integer division 5 / 2 is 2 ; use 5 / 2.0 or cast to double . Next: look up a term in the glossary , browse the full reference , or test a snippet in the Try It Editor ."}]},{"page":"c-glossary.html","title":"C Glossary","group":"Reference","url":"c-glossary.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Glossary","text":"C Tutorial › Reference › Glossary C Glossary Short, practical definitions of the words you meet while learning C. Use the filter to find a term instantly. A B C D E F G H I K L M N O P R S T U V W"},{"id":"letter-a","h":"A","text":"Address A number that identifies a byte in memory. The & operator obtains the address of a variable; a pointer stores one. Argument A value passed to a function at the call site. The matching variable inside the function is the parameter . The words are often used interchangeably. Array A fixed-size block of consecutive elements of one type. Indexes start at 0. An array name decays to a pointer to its first element when passed to a function. ASCII A character encoding that maps 0–127 to letters, digits, punctuation and control codes. For example 'A' is 65 and '0' is 48. Assembly Low-level human-readable machine instructions. C compilers translate source code to assembly or directly to object code. auto The default storage class for local variables. In modern C, auto is almost never written explicitly."},{"id":"letter-b","h":"B","text":"Binary Base-2 notation using only 0 and 1. One binary digit is a bit; eight bits make a byte. Bit The smallest unit of data: 0 or 1. Bitwise operators work directly on individual bits. Bitwise operator & AND, | OR, ^ XOR, ~ NOT, and shifts << / >> . They operate on the binary representation of integers. Block A group of statements inside braces { } . A block introduces a new scope. Buffer A region of memory used to hold data temporarily, commonly an array receiving input or file contents. Buffer overflow Writing past the end of an array or buffer. It corrupts neighbouring memory and is a classic security bug. Use bounded functions and check lengths. Byte Eight bits. sizeof(char) is always 1 byte in C."},{"id":"letter-c","h":"C","text":"Call stack The runtime structure that holds one stack frame per active function call, including parameters, locals and the return address. Cast Explicit conversion between types, written (double)n or (int)3.9 . Casting does not change the original variable. char The smallest integer type, usually one byte. It stores a character code or a small integer. A string is an array of char ending in '\\0' . Compiler A program that translates C source code into object code, then links it into an executable. GCC, Clang and MSVC are common C compilers. const A qualifier promising that a value will not be modified through that name. const int *p means the pointed-to value is read-only; int *const p means the pointer itself is fixed. Constant A value that cannot change, such as 42 , 'A' , 3.14 , or a name defined with #define / const ."},{"id":"letter-d","h":"D","text":"Dangling pointer A pointer that still holds the address of memory that has been freed or gone out of scope. Using it is undefined behaviour — set pointers to NULL after free . Data type A category that determines the size, representation and allowed operations of a value: int , double , char , pointers, structs and so on. Declaration Tells the compiler that a name exists and what type it has, without necessarily allocating storage. A definition creates it. Dereference Accessing the value a pointer points to with *p . Dereferencing NULL or an invalid pointer crashes the program. Directive A line beginning with # handled by the preprocessor, such as #include , #define and #if . double A double-precision floating-point type, usually 8 bytes and about 15 significant decimal digits."},{"id":"letter-e","h":"E","text":"enum A type whose values are named integer constants: enum Color { RED, GREEN, BLUE }; . The names are easier to read than raw numbers. Escape sequence A backslash combination representing a special character: \\n newline, \\t tab, \\\\ backslash, \\0 null. Expression A combination of values, variables, operators and function calls that produces a value, such as a + b * 2 ."},{"id":"letter-f","h":"F","text":"File pointer A FILE * returned by fopen and used with fread , fprintf , fclose and related functions. float A single-precision floating-point type, usually 4 bytes and about 6 significant digits. Use double unless memory is tight. Function A named, reusable block of code with a return type and optional parameters. Every C program starts at main . Function pointer A pointer that stores the address of a function, allowing callbacks and dispatch tables: int (*op)(int, int) = add; ."},{"id":"letter-g","h":"G","text":"Garbage value An unpredictable value in an uninitialised variable. Local variables are not automatically zeroed — initialise them. Global variable A variable declared outside all functions. It is visible from its declaration to the end of the file and lives for the whole program."},{"id":"letter-h","h":"H","text":"Header A .h file containing declarations, macros and type definitions, included with #include . Heap The region used for dynamic allocation with malloc , calloc and realloc . You manage its lifetime with free . Hexadecimal Base-16 notation using 0–9 and A–F, written with a 0x prefix: 0xFF is 255. Common for memory addresses and bit masks."},{"id":"letter-i","h":"I","text":"Identifier A name for a variable, function, type or label. It must begin with a letter or underscore and contain only letters, digits and underscores. Include guard #ifndef NAME_H / #define NAME_H / #endif around a header, preventing it from being included twice. Initialisation Giving a variable its first value at the point of definition: int n = 0; . Distinct from later assignment. Integer A whole number with no fractional part, such as int , long or unsigned short . Interpreter A program that executes source code directly instead of producing a native executable. The Try It Editor uses a small C interpreter (PicoC) compiled to WebAssembly."},{"id":"letter-k","h":"K","text":"Keyword A word reserved by the language, such as int , return , while or struct . You cannot use it as an identifier."},{"id":"letter-l","h":"L","text":"Library A collection of compiled functions and declarations that can be linked into your program, such as the C standard library or a maths library. Linker The tool that combines object files and libraries into an executable, resolving references between them. Literal A value written directly in source code: 42 , 3.14 , 'x' , \"hello\" . Local variable A variable declared inside a block or function. It exists only while that block executes. Loop A construct that repeats statements: for , while or do...while . Lvalue An expression that refers to a storage location and can appear on the left of an assignment, such as a variable or *p ."},{"id":"letter-m","h":"M","text":"Macro A preprocessor name replaced textually before compilation, defined with #define . Parenthesise macro parameters carefully. main The entry point of a C program. It returns an int status to the operating system; 0 means success. Memory leak Memory that was allocated but never freed, so the program keeps consuming memory until it exits."},{"id":"letter-n","h":"N","text":"Null pointer A pointer with the value NULL , meaning it points to nothing. Check before dereferencing. Null terminator The '\\0' character that marks the end of a C string. Functions such as printf(\"%s\") and strlen rely on it."},{"id":"letter-o","h":"O","text":"Operand A value or expression that an operator acts on. In a + b , both a and b are operands. Operator A symbol that performs an operation: arithmetic, comparison, logic, assignment, bitwise or member access. Overflow When a value is too large for its type. Signed integer overflow is undefined behaviour; unsigned arithmetic wraps modulo 2 n ."},{"id":"letter-p","h":"P","text":"Parameter A variable declared in a function definition that receives an argument from the caller. Pointer A variable that holds a memory address. Its type says what kind of value lives at that address. Declared with int *p . Preprocessor The first translation phase. It handles #include , #define and conditional compilation before the compiler sees the code. Prototype A function declaration that gives the name, return type and parameter types, allowing calls before the definition appears."},{"id":"letter-r","h":"R","text":"Recursion A function calling itself. Every recursive function needs a base case that stops the recursion, or the stack will overflow. Return value The value a function sends back to its caller with return . A function returning void returns no value."},{"id":"letter-s","h":"S","text":"Scope The part of a program where a name is visible. Block scope ends at } ; file scope lasts to the end of the translation unit. Segmentation fault A crash caused by accessing memory the process is not allowed to touch — often a NULL or wild pointer, or an out-of-bounds access. Signed An integer type that can hold negative and positive values. The default for int is signed. Stack The region that stores function call frames and local variables. It grows and shrinks automatically; large local arrays can overflow it. Statement A complete instruction, usually ending in a semicolon: a declaration, assignment, call, loop or jump. static At file scope, gives a name internal linkage (private to the file). Inside a function, gives a local variable a lifetime for the whole program. String A sequence of characters ending in '\\0' , stored in a char array. There is no separate string type in C. struct A composite type grouping named members, possibly of different types, into one object. Syntax The grammar rules of the language. A syntax error means the compiler cannot parse the code."},{"id":"letter-t","h":"T","text":"Token The smallest lexical unit: a keyword, identifier, literal, operator or punctuation mark. typedef Creates an alias for a type: typedef unsigned long ulong; . It does not create a new type."},{"id":"letter-u","h":"U","text":"Undefined behaviour Code the C standard does not define — for example signed overflow, out-of-bounds access or double free. The compiler may do anything; the program is not reliable. union A type whose members share the same memory. Only one member holds a meaningful value at a time. Useful for tagged variants and low-level representations. unsigned An integer type that cannot be negative. Unsigned arithmetic wraps around instead of overflowing."},{"id":"letter-v","h":"V","text":"Variable A named storage location with a type. Its value can change during execution. void “No type”: a function returning void returns nothing; void * is a generic object pointer that converts to any object pointer type. volatile A qualifier telling the compiler that a value may change at any time, so it must not cache or optimise away accesses. Used for hardware registers and signal flags, not for thread synchronisation."},{"id":"letter-w","h":"W","text":"Warning A diagnostic that is probably a mistake but still valid C. Compile with -Wall -Wextra and fix warnings before they become bugs. No term matches that filter."}]},{"page":"c-examples.html","title":"C Examples","group":"Reference","url":"c-examples.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"C Examples","text":"C Tutorial › Examples C Examples Complete, compilable programs. Load any of them into the editor with Try it , or compile them locally with gcc file.c -o file ."},{"id":"1-hello-world","h":"1. Hello, World","text":"Output"},{"id":"2-add-two-numbers","h":"2. Add two numbers","text":"Output"},{"id":"3-even-or-odd","h":"3. Even or odd","text":"Output"},{"id":"4-largest-of-three-numbers","h":"4. Largest of three numbers","text":"Output"},{"id":"5-multiplication-table","h":"5. Multiplication table","text":"Output"},{"id":"6-factorial","h":"6. Factorial","text":"Output"},{"id":"7-prime-numbers-below-50","h":"7. Prime numbers below 50","text":"Output"},{"id":"8-reverse-a-string","h":"8. Reverse a string","text":"Output"},{"id":"9-average-of-an-array","h":"9. Average of an array","text":"Output"},{"id":"10-sort-an-array-bubble-sort","h":"10. Sort an array (bubble sort)","text":"Output"},{"id":"11-count-vowels","h":"11. Count vowels","text":"Output"},{"id":"12-student-record-with-a-structure","h":"12. Student record with a structure","text":"Output"},{"id":"13-swap-with-pointers","h":"13. Swap with pointers","text":"Output"},{"id":"14-write-and-read-a-file","h":"14. Write and read a file","text":"Output"},{"id":"15-dynamic-array-with-malloc","h":"15. Dynamic array with malloc","text":"Output"},{"id":"more-practice","h":"More practice","text":"Take the quiz to check your understanding. Work through the exercises at the end of each chapter. Rebuild these examples without looking, then compare. Try the same program with input instead of hard-coded values."}]},{"page":"tryit.html","title":"Try It Editor","group":"Tools","url":"tryit.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"Try It Editor","text":"C Tutorial › Try It Editor Try It Editor Write C code, press Run , and see real output — no internet connection, no account, and nothing sent to a server. The editor uses PicoC , a small C interpreter compiled to WebAssembly. main.c Open .c Copy Download .c Reset ▶ Run Program input (stdin) — used by scanf, fgets and getchar ▶ Run Show sample output Press Run to execute your code. Ctrl/⌘ + Enter Output sample output Want a native executable? Use Download .c , then run gcc main.c -o main and ./main (or main.exe on Windows). See Get Started for compiler installation. About the built-in engine. PicoC is an interpreter for a practical subset of C89/C99. It supports printf , scanf , arrays, strings, pointers, structs, unions, enums, recursion, malloc , file-style I/O and most of the standard library. A few valid C features — such as aggregate initialisers for structs, very complex macros and variable-length arrays — are not supported. For full standard C, compile with GCC or Clang. PicoC is BSD-licensed. See attribution . Runs are limited to 10 seconds; infinite loops are stopped automatically."}]},{"page":"search.html","title":"Search","group":"Tools","url":"search.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"Search","text":"C Tutorial › Search Search C Notes Find a topic, function or term across every chapter, example, exercise and reference table. Search runs entirely in your browser. ⌕ All Tutorial Advanced Practice Reference Tools Loading the search index… No results. Try a shorter word, a function name such as malloc , or browse the chapters in the sidebar. pointer malloc printf struct fopen array undefined behaviour Tip: press Ctrl + K from any page to open this search, and Enter to jump to the first result."}]},{"page":"bookmarks.html","title":"Bookmarks","group":"Tools","url":"bookmarks.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"Bookmarks","text":"C Tutorial › Tools › Bookmarks Bookmarks Pages you have starred are collected here. Bookmarks are stored in this browser only — no account, no syncing, no tracking. No bookmarks yet. Press the ☆ button in the top bar, or B , on any page to save it here. Clear all bookmarks Looking for something else? Try the full-text search , the cheat sheet , or the exercise list ."}]},{"page":"404.html","title":"Page not found","group":"Other","url":"404.html","sections":[{"id":"","h":"Page not found","text":"404 That page is not here The link may be out of date, or the file may have been moved. Try one of these instead: C Tutorial home Search all content Cheat sheet"}]}];
